title: Live-verify Custom STT + Custom TTS against a real OpenAI-compatible gateway (dogfood)
updated_at: "2026-08-17T05:25:04Z"
---
After B174 (Custom STT) and B182 (Custom TTS) land, verify both clients end-to-end against a live OpenAI-compatible audio gateway using a Consumer Bearer key.

## Steps
1. Pick base (probe /models with the host key on both public CF and mesh origins; pin whichever returns 200 — keys are origin-scoped, never overwrite a host key with another's).
2. Custom STT: set stt.provider=custom, custom_base_url, key (prefer non-inline: api_key_file or api_key_command), custom_model for /audio/transcriptions (or custom_path=/stt dual-mount where model is optional). Generate a test WAV (speech, not silence — a 440Hz tone returns empty text but 200; use real or TTS-generated speech), run hark listen / hark ask, assert non-empty transcript + provider=custom.
3. Custom TTS: set tts.provider=custom, custom_base_url, key, custom_model + voice, run hark tts 'hello world', assert audible playback / mp3 bytes written, provider=custom.
4. Record evidence: HTTP codes, transcript text, audio byte counts, provider names in any output.
5. Classify gateway 503 control_plane_unavailable as transient (retry ~2min) — not a client bug. READY only after both surfaces return 2xx with real payloads.

## Acceptance
- Both providers return real payloads (non-empty transcript; >0 audio bytes with audio content-type) with provider=custom reported.
- No secret printed in logs/output — key from file/command source, never echoed.